        { "text": "$\\dfrac{1}{2} - \\dfrac{1}{2}\\cos 12x$", "why": "Used the $\\sin^{2}$ form of the identity, which carries a minus sign." },
        { "text": "$\\dfrac{1}{2} + \\dfrac{1}{2}\\cos 6x$", "why": "Did not double the angle when applying $\\cos 2A$ with $A = 6x$." },
        { "text": "$\\dfrac{1}{2} - \\dfrac{1}{2}\\cos 6x$", "why": "Used the $\\sin^{2}$ form and also failed to double the angle." }
      ],
      "solution_text": "$\\cos 2A = 2\\cos^{2}A - 1$\nWith $A = 6x$:\n$\\cos 12x = 2\\cos^{2}6x - 1$\n$2\\cos^{2}6x = 1 + \\cos 12x$\n$\\cos^{2}6x = \\dfrac{1}{2} + \\dfrac{1}{2}\\cos 12x$"
    },
